WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:11.300
In healthcare, large amounts of heterogeneous
medical data have become available in various

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.900
healthcare organizations.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.580
This data could be an enabling resource for
deriving insights for improving care delivery

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.700
and reducing waste.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.850
The enormity and complexity of these datasets
present great challenges in analyses and subsequent

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.090
applications to a practical clinical environment.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:35.579
In this series of lectures, we will explore
the intersection of healthcare and big data.

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:39.899
At the end of this lecture, you should develop
understanding of healthcare data, the different

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:45.079
analytics algorithms, and understanding the
big data systems.

00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:50.129
The healthcare industry is huge, and there
are a lot of data coming out of healthcare.

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.820
The United States healthcare is incredibly
expensive.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.039
Overall spending is $3.8 trillion in U.S.
per year.

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:02.920
That’s more than the value of ten biggest
companies plus ten Beijing Olympics plus a

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.800
Warren Buffett, and a Bill Gates.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.090
But does it have to be that way?

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.560
There is massive waste in healthcare unfortunately.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.409
The estimated waste in U.S. healthcare alone
is $765 billion.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:21.840
That’s equivalent to the NASA’s total
budget for the past 50 years.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.810
Not only the cost is an issue, but also the
quality of healthcare is poor.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.620
There are 200 to 400 thousand preventable
deaths per year in the U.S.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.580
That’s 1,000 people per day.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.140
If we classify the preventable deaths against
other causes of deaths, it will be the number

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.289
three causes of deaths in the United States,
after only heart disease and cancer.

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:49.259
So, there are massive problems presented in
modern healthcare, Including high costs, high

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:51.590
waste, and low quality.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.840
How can big data help?

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.729
The hope is big data can lead to better care
and lower cost.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:02.799
In big data, people talk about the four V’s:
&nbsp;

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:07.170
Volume - Healthcare generates a large volume
of data.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:13.630
For example, for genomic data each human genome
requires 200 gigabytes of raw data or 125

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.300
megabytes, if we store just SNPs.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.870
For medical imaging data, a single MRI is
about 300 gigabytes.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:28.690
Medical imaging data generated in the U.S.
per year was estimated to be 100 petabyte.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.730
&nbsp;
Variety - Healthcare also generates a lot

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.440
of different kind of information.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.110
Such as clinical information, including patient’s
demographics, diagnosis, procedure, medication,

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:43.480
lab results, and the clinical notes.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:48.160
Patient also generated a lot of data, such
as information coming out of arm body sensors

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.080
and other devices that patients wear.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.170
&nbsp;
Velocity - Healthcare also generate a lot

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:59.500
of real time data, such as blood pressure
measures, temperature, heart rate, drug dispensing

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.340
levels at intensive care units.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.690
&nbsp;
Veracity - Unfortunately, healthcare data

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:10.410
often comes with a lot of noise, a lot of
missing data, a lot of errors, and a lot of

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.400
false alarms.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:05:51.520
It is a big challenge 

00:05:51.520 --> 00:06:25.889
in 

00:06:25.889 --> 00:07:23.099
healthcare 

00:07:23.099 --> 00:09:43.449
data analytics.

